KP = sorted(df(E5L)[df(E5L)['sample'] == 'pooled'].n_train_women.unique()); K1, K2, K3 = float(KP[0]), float(KP[1]), float(KP[2]); kc = lambda k: calc('learning-curve k', int(k), '{:.0f}', E5L, 'unique n_train_women')
kmax = lambda s_: float(max(df(E5L)[df(E5L)["sample"] == s_].n_train_women)); k20 = lambda s_: 20.0
lcv = lambda s_, t_, f_, k_: float(df(E5L).query("sample == @s_ and target == @t_ and fset == @f_ and n_train_women == @k_").R2_oos.iloc[0])
a(f"The last column of each table (k = all other women) reproduces the Phase 4 result exactly (largest difference {agg(E5I, '', 'abs_diff', 'max', '{:.1e}', label='E5 learning-curve identity check')}). **Reading.** (1) With very few women (k = {kc(K1)}) the questionnaire model is not better than the mean (DRSP, pooled, R2 {ci(E5L, dict(sample='pooled', target='drsp_psych', fset='D', n_train_women=K1), 'R2_oos', '{:+.2f}', label='E5 LC D pooled drsp k=10')}) and its training R2 ({val(E5L, dict(sample='pooled', target='drsp_psych', fset='D', n_train_women=K1), 'train_R2', '{:.2f}', label='E5 LC train D pooled drsp k=10')}) is far above its test R2: overfitting. (2) By k = {kc(K2)} the test R2 has jumped to {ci(E5L, dict(sample='pooled', target='drsp_psych', fset='D', n_train_women=K2), 'R2_oos', '{:+.2f}', label='E5 LC D pooled drsp k=20')} and then changes slowly up to all {calc('E5 k max pooled', kmax('pooled') + 1, '{:.0f}', E5L, 'largest k + 1 held-out woman')} women ({ci(E5L, dict(sample='pooled', target='drsp_psych', fset='D', n_train_women=kmax('pooled')), 'R2_oos', '{:+.2f}', label='E5 LC D pooled drsp kmax')}), while the training R2 falls ({val(E5L, dict(sample='pooled', target='drsp_psych', fset='D', n_train_women=kmax('pooled')), 'train_R2', '{:.2f}', label='E5 LC train D pooled drsp kmax')} at the end): the gap closes and the curve is flat, so for this ridge model on these predictors more women of the same kind would add little. (3) The other two targets show no systematic further rise beyond k = {kc(K3)} either (see the tables), within wide intervals; for phase alone (set A) the gain with more women is small (DRSP, pooled: {val(E5L, dict(sample='pooled', target='drsp_psych', fset='A', n_train_women=K1), 'R2_oos', '{:+.3f}', label='E5 LC A pooled drsp k=10')} at k = {kc(K1)} to {val(E5L, dict(sample='pooled', target='drsp_psych', fset='A', n_train_women=kmax('pooled')), 'R2_oos', '{:+.3f}', label='E5 LC A pooled drsp kmax')} with all women), and the phase-plus-hormones set C does not improve with more women either (DRSP, pooled, k = all: {val(E5L, dict(sample='pooled', target='drsp_psych', fset='C', n_train_women=kmax('pooled')), 'R2_oos', '{:+.3f}', label='E5 LC C pooled drsp kmax')}). Caveats: single random draw of the training women per point, intervals overlap between neighbouring k, and the curve cannot be extrapolated beyond {calc('E5 k max pooled again', kmax('pooled'), '{:.0f}', E5L, 'largest k')} training women. Figure: `results/fig_E5_learning_curves.png`.\n")
